      "text": "Swartz Creek was first settled in June 1836 by Adam Miller and his eleven‑child family in what was then Flint Township.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Swartz_Creek%2C_Michigan",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 1881 the community gained a rail station called Hamilton, served by the Goodyear and Miller elevator and Western Union Telegraph.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        19,
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Swartz_Creek%2C_Michigan",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Swartz Creek Area Historical Society, founded in 1971, operates a museum and research center inside the historic Masonic Temple downtown.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://museumsdatabase.com/museums/view/16071",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The society also preserves historic structures such as the Swartz Creek Depot and the Old Union Schoolhouse.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://museumsdatabase.com/museums/view/16071",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "In 2004 the city established a downtown development authority to revitalize its historic core.",
